---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArcMotif from '../components/ArcMotif.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url.js';
---
<Layout title="Research" description="Geometry of Happiness — how a mathematical mind learned to design for human experience." slug="research">
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <span class="eyebrow">Research</span>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,2.8rem); margin-top:0.5rem;">Geometry of Happiness</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most people shake sand out of their shoes and move on. I wondered why something so small
      could change the way a person walked — and that question turned into three years of
      independently designed research, each project a size larger than the last, that kept
      pulling me toward architecture.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="arc-divider"><ArcMotif size={48} /></div>

  <section class="section section--tight stack">
    <article>
      <span class="eyebrow">Study 01 — Nov–Dec 2025 — Object</span>
      <h3 style="margin-top:0.3rem;">Relationship Between Shoe Size and the Volume of Sand Entering the Shoe</h3>
      <p>
        It started with a math modeling paper. I treated the structure of a shoe, the size of the
        particles, the friction, the walking pattern, as variables I could measure and reduce —
        ending in a derived formula for sand and gravel accumulation against shoe size. Good
        analysis meant a more accurate model. Happiness, at the time, was close to calculable
        comfort.
      </p>
      <div class="case-study__tags">
        <span class="tag">Variables: shoe structure</span>
        <span class="tag">particle size</span>
        <span class="tag">friction</span>
        <span class="tag">gait pattern</span>
      </div>
      <p class="pull-quote" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        "I liked that math could turn irritation into order."
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
        Now continuing as two live threads: a collaboration with Dr. Hemant Kumar Singh
        (Department of Mathematics, University of Delhi) on the elevator-circulation extension
        below, and a window/light research project this summer at Princeton. Awarded 3rd Prize
        at the International Youth Research Paper Competition, presented by the President of the
        Education Association for Software Empowerment, and published on an open-access journal.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <span class="eyebrow">Study 02 — Building</span>
      <h3 style="margin-top:0.3rem;">Elevator Circulation: From Wait Time to Shared Discomfort</h3>
      <p>
        The next project moved from an object to a building. An elevator circulation study —
        floor counts, wait times, time-of-day traffic, how many people boarded at once — looked
        like the same kind of numbers. But the purpose had shifted: not shrinking a number, but
        shrinking a resident's impatience.
      </p>
      <div class="case-study__tags">
        <span class="tag">Variables: floor count</span>
        <span class="tag">wait time</span>
        <span class="tag">traffic pattern</span>
        <span class="tag">occupancy</span>
      </div>
      <p class="pull-quote" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        "An elevator delay was not just thirty seconds. It was thirty seconds of shifting
        weight, checked watches, and quiet impatience."
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <span class="eyebrow">Study 03 — Room</span>
      <h3 style="margin-top:0.3rem;">A Window, Logged</h3>
      <p>
        The current project is the smallest in scale and the largest in what it asks. I started
        recording the size of my dorm window, the angle of light, the length of the afternoon
        shadow across my desk. The question changed on its own, from <em>how much light enters</em>
        to <em>how does this light make me feel</em>.
      </p>
      <div class="case-study__tags">
        <span class="tag">Variables: window size</span>
        <span class="tag">light angle</span>
        <span class="tag">illuminance</span>
        <span class="tag">shadow length</span>
      </div>
      <p class="pull-quote" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        "The window became less a rectangle of glass than a measure of how much of the outside
        world I was allowed to feel."
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <span class="eyebrow">Future — Architecture</span>
      <h3 style="margin-top:0.3rem;">The Design of Happiness</h3>
      <p>
        I haven't abandoned math — I've learned that happiness in a space also speaks in light,
        silence, proportion, and mood, languages a formula alone can't write. The sand in my shoe
        first taught me to measure discomfort. Architecture now asks more of me: to design the
        conditions in which comfort, attention, and happiness can quietly take root. See where
        this has already landed on <a href={withBase('design-work')}>Art &amp; Architecture</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="arc-divider"><ArcMotif size={48} /></div>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <span class="eyebrow">Field Notes</span>
    <ul class="awards-list" style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">Phillips Exeter Academy</span><span class="award-detail">Exeter, NH — Class of 2027</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">GPA 10.4/12.0</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">SAT</span><span class="award-detail">Reading &amp; Writing 760 · Math 800</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">1560</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">AMC 12</span><span class="award-detail">American Mathematics Competition — 12A: 96 · 12B: 93</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">2025 · 2026</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">AIME I</span><span class="award-detail">American Invitational Mathematics Examination — Score: 5</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">2026</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">Math Textbook</span><span class="award-detail">Wrote a math workbook expanding on my experience tutoring math at Exeter — forthcoming</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">2026</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">USA Invitational Young Physicists Tournament</span><span class="award-detail">3rd place — team research on the composition and physics of flash bulbs</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">2026</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Layout>
